---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import WhatsAppCTA from '../../components/WhatsAppCTA.astro';
import QuickFacts from '../../components/QuickFacts.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';
import { BLOG_POSTS } from '../../data/blog';

const post = BLOG_POSTS[0];
const title = `${post.title} | Tours Antalya Blog`;
const description = post.description;
const heroImage = post.image;

const blogSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BlogPosting',
  headline: post.title,
  description: post.description,
  datePublished: post.date,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: post.author },
  publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name, url: SITE.url },
  url: `${SITE.url}/blog/${post.slug}/`,
  image: post.image,
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE.url },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Blog', item: `${SITE.url}/blog/` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: post.title, item: `${SITE.url}/blog/${post.slug}/` },
  ],
};

const otherPosts = BLOG_POSTS.filter((p) => p.slug !== post.slug);
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} schema={[blogSchema, breadcrumbSchema]} canonical={`/blog/${post.slug}/`} type="article" image={heroImage}>
  <section class="relative flex min-h-[40vh] items-end overflow-hidden">
    <div class="absolute inset-0 z-0">
      <img src={heroImage} alt={post.title} class="h-full w-full object-cover" fetchpriority="high" width="1920" height="1080" />
      <div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-primary-950/95 via-primary-950/60 to-primary-950/30"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="relative z-10 mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-16 lg:px-8">
      <span class="inline-block rounded-full bg-white/10 px-4 py-1.5 text-sm font-medium text-primary-200 backdrop-blur-sm">
        {post.category}
      </span>
      <h1 class="mt-6 font-display text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-white sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl">
        {post.title}
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-primary-200">
        {new Date(post.date).toLocaleDateString('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long', day: 'numeric' })} &middot; {post.readTime} &middot; By {post.author}
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Breadcrumb items={[
    { name: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { name: 'Blog', href: '/blog/' },
    { name: post.title, href: `/blog/${post.slug}/` },
  ]} />

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12 lg:px-8">
    <QuickFacts
      title="Key Takeaways"
      items={[
        { label: 'Location', value: 'Antalya Province, southern Turkey, Mediterranean coast' },
        { label: 'Population', value: 'Approximately 2.7 million' },
        { label: 'Coastline', value: '640 km of Mediterranean shoreline' },
        { label: 'Climate', value: 'Hot summers, mild winters, 300+ sunny days' },
        { label: 'History', value: 'Inhabited since Paleolithic era, Roman, Byzantine, Ottoman' },
        { label: 'Top attractions', value: 'Kaleiçi, Düden Falls, Aspendos, Perge, beaches' },
      ]}
    />
  </div>

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 pb-12 lg:px-8">
    <h2 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold text-neutral-900">Introduction to Antalya Province</h2>
    <p class="mt-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-neutral-700">
      Antalya Province is Turkey\'s tourism crown jewel, a vast region stretching along 640
      kilometers of Mediterranean coastline in the country\'s southwest. With a population of
      approximately 2.7 million and an annual influx of over 15 million international visitors,
      Antalya is one of the world\'s most visited tourism destinations. The province encompasses
      diverse landscapes, from the sun-drenched beaches of Lara and Konyaaltı to the pine-clad
      mountains of the Taurus range, from the ancient ruins of Perge and Aspendos to the
      waterfalls of Düden and Kurşunlu. This complete guide covers everything you need to know
      about Antalya Province: its history, culture, beaches, climate, and top experiences.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-3xl font-bold text-neutral-900">A Journey Through History</h2>
    <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-700">
      Antalya\'s history stretches back to the Paleolithic era, with evidence of human settlement
      dating back 200,000 years in the Karain Cave. The region has been shaped by successive
      civilizations: the Lycians, who built rock tombs along the coast; the Persians, who
      conquered the region in the 6th century BC; Alexander the Great, who passed through in 333
      BC; the Romans, who built the magnificent cities of Perge, Aspendos, and Side; the
      Byzantines, who left their mark in churches and fortifications; the Seljuks, who
      constructed the iconic Yivli Minaret; and the Ottomans, who ruled for nearly 500 years
      until the foundation of the Turkish Republic in 1923.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-700">
      Each civilization left layers of architecture, culture, and cuisine that make Antalya a
      living museum. The old town of Kaleiçi, with its Roman walls, Ottoman houses, and Seljuk
      minarets, encapsulates this layered history in a walkable district. The ancient Roman
      theater at Aspendos, built in the 2nd century AD, remains one of the best-preserved
      theaters in the world and still hosts performances today. The ruined city of Perge, with
      its colonnaded streets, Roman baths, and stadium, offers a glimpse into urban life in
      antiquity.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-3xl font-bold text-neutral-900">Culture & Traditions</h2>
    <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-700">
      Antalya\'s culture reflects its position at the crossroads of Mediterranean and Anatolian
      civilizations. The local cuisine is a highlight, featuring fresh Mediterranean ingredients,
      olive oil dishes, grilled meats, and an abundance of seafood. Signature dishes include
      <em>piyaz</em> (a white bean salad with tahini), <em>şiş köfte</em> (grilled meat skewers),
      and <em>burnt ice cream</em> (a traditional Turkish dessert). The region is also known for
      its citrus fruits, pomegranates, and local wines.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-700">
      Traditional arts in Antalya include carpet weaving, copper work, and wood carving, all of
      which can be found in the workshops and bazaars of Kaleiçi. The Antalya Culture Center
      hosts regular performances of Turkish classical music, folk dancing, and theater. The
      Aspendos International Opera and Ballet Festival, held annually in the ancient Roman
      theater, is a unique cultural experience that combines world-class performance with
      2,000-year-old architecture.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-3xl font-bold text-neutral-900">Beaches of Antalya Province</h2>
    <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-700">
      The beaches of Antalya Province are its most famous attraction, ranging from long sandy
      stretches to hidden coves accessible only by boat. <strong>Lara Beach</strong>, just east
      of Antalya city, is a 12-kilometer stretch of golden sand lined with luxury all-inclusive
      resorts. A <strong>5-star hotel reservation in Lara region directly by the sea</strong>
      offers direct beach access and stunning sunrise views. <strong>Konyaaltı Beach</strong>, on
      the city\'s western side, is a pebbled beach with mountain views and a vibrant beach club
      scene. A <strong>Konyaaltı beach guesthouse Antalya</strong> stay provides a more local and
      intimate experience.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-700">
      Further along the coast, <strong>Belek</strong> is known for its golf resorts and pristine
      Blue Flag beaches. <strong>Side</strong> offers the unique experience of swimming beside
      ancient Roman ruins. <strong>Kemer</strong> features dramatic mountain-backed pebbled
      coves. <strong>Alanya</strong>, at the eastern edge of the province, boasts Cleopatra Beach,
      said to have been a gift from Mark Antony to the Egyptian queen. Each beach has its own
      character, and our team can recommend the perfect one based on your preferences.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-3xl font-bold text-neutral-900">Climate & Best Time to Visit</h2>
    <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-700">
      Antalya enjoys a typical Mediterranean climate with hot, dry summers and mild, rainy
      winters. Summer temperatures (June to September) range from 30°C to 40°C, with sea
      temperatures reaching 28°C. Spring (April to June) and autumn (September to November) are
      ideal for sightseeing, with temperatures between 20°C and 30°C and lower humidity. Winter
      (December to March) sees temperatures of 10°C to 15°C, pleasant for walking and exploring
      ancient sites without the crowds.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-700">
      For beach holidays, the best time is June to September. For city tours and archaeological
      visits, April to June and September to November offer the most comfortable conditions. For
      golf, the season runs from September to May when temperatures are moderate. Business
      travelers will find the region accessible year-round, with the added benefit of lower hotel
      rates outside the peak summer season.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-12 rounded-2xl bg-primary-50 p-8 text-center">
      <h3 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-900">Plan Your Antalya Trip</h3>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base text-neutral-600">Contact us on WhatsApp for personalized travel advice and bookings.</p>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
        <WhatsAppCTA label="Plan Your Trip" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-16 font-display text-2xl font-bold text-neutral-900">More Articles</h2>
    <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2">
      {otherPosts.map((p) => (
        <a href={`/blog/${p.slug}/`} class="group flex items-center gap-4 rounded-xl border border-neutral-200 p-4 transition-all hover:shadow-md">
          <div class="h-16 w-16 flex-shrink-0 overflow-hidden rounded-lg">
            <img src={p.image} alt={p.title} class="h-full w-full object-cover" loading="lazy" width="200" height="200" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-neutral-900 group-hover:text-primary-600">{p.title}</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-neutral-500">{p.readTime}</p>
          </div>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
